#!/bin/bash
# system-prompt hook: teach models about side-call CLIs installed in dotfiles.
#
# 1. `vision`  — image transcription via a vision-capable model
#    (Command Code VISION pattern, commandcode.ai/docs/vision): a text-only
#    model side-calls a cheap vision-capable model to describe images,
#    instead of switching session models.
# 2. `websearch` — live web search via the TinyFish Search API
#    (docs.tinyfish.ai/search-api): structured results (titles, URLs,
#    snippets) as grounding, without a web-capable session model.
#
# stdin:  current system prompt
# stdout: prompt + tool appendices

appendix='

## VISION tool (image reading side-call)

You have access to the `vision` CLI (on PATH). It sends an image to a
vision-capable model and returns a text transcription. Use it whenever you
need to see an image and cannot do so natively.

How to call:
  vision <image-path-or-url> ["question"]        # plain text answer
  vision --json <image>                          # JSON: answer + usage
  vision a.png b.png "compare these two"         # multiple images
  vision --probe                                 # which models see images

Rules (mirroring Command Code semantics):
- Reading an image is YOUR decision, mid-turn, like reading a file. Nothing
  is sent until you call it.
- It is independent of the session model — it never changes which model
  answers the prompt. Do not suggest switching models.
- Ask for what is IN the image: "what is the exact error text" works;
  "does this match what we changed" does not (the transcriber has no context).
- The call is bounded by a timeout and FAILS OPEN: on failure, continue the
  turn and report the stderr reason in one line, e.g.
  "VISION failed (timeout after 90s) — continuing without it."
- Prefer --json when you want machine-checkable output.

When NOT to call it:
- Pure text tasks, code reading, anything not requiring image content.
- If the user asks "what is in this image" but gave no path/URL, ask for one
  rather than guessing.

## WEB_SEARCH tool (live web search side-call)

You have access to the `websearch` CLI (on PATH). It searches the live web
via the TinyFish Search API and returns structured results (titles, URLs,
snippets, dates). Use it whenever you need facts that depend on what is true
NOW: current versions/pricing, recent releases or news, library docs you
are unsure about, anything past your training cutoff or beyond this repo.

How to call:
  websearch <query>                        # rendered results (default 8)
  websearch <query> --json                 # raw payload (machine-readable)
  websearch <query> --news                 # news w/ publisher+date
  websearch <query> --papers               # academic papers
  websearch <query> --recent 1440          # last 24h (minutes)
  websearch <query> --after 2025-01-01     # date-window filters
  websearch "query" --include github.com   # domain restrict/exclude
  websearch "query" -l DE --lang de        # locale (default US/en)
  websearch --status                       # key/diagnostics check

Rules (same spirit as VISION):
- Searching is YOUR decision, mid-turn, like reading a file. Nothing is
  sent until you call it; queries leave the VM (privacy applies).
- Independent of the session model; never suggest switching models.
- Search is free on TinyFish (30 req/min). Still: one well-formed query beats
  five spray-and-pray queries; add --purpose "<intent>" for terse keyword
  queries to improve relevance.
- Snippets are leads, not truth: corroborate across results or read the page
  (curl the URL) before asserting a fact.
- Cite result URLs in your final answer when results informed it.
- FAILS OPEN: on error, report the stderr reason in one line and continue
  without web grounding (e.g. "WEB_SEARCH failed (401: invalid key)").
- `websearch --status` shows which backend answers. Keyless here via the
  exe.dev edge proxy; elsewhere set TINYFISH_API_KEY — never ask the user to
  paste a secret into chat.

When NOT to call it:
- Anything answerable from the local repo, this VM, or stable well-known
  knowledge. Local-first: grep the codebase before querying the web.'

printf '%s\n%s\n' "$(cat)" "$appendix"
